S={"country":"New Zealand","src":{"label":"Ministry for Primary Industries (MPI) — step-by-step guide to bringing cats and dogs to NZ (2026 process)","url":"https://www.mpi.govt.nz/bring-send-to-nz/pets-travelling-to-nz/bringing-cats-and-dogs-to-nz/bringing-cats-and-dogs-to-nz-using-the-2026-import-health-standard/step-by-step-guide-to-bringing-cats-and-dogs-to-nz-2026"},
"head":"A new import health standard for cats and dogs took effect on 1 July 2026; MPI recommends a pet transport company.",
"lines":["MPI says the Cats and Dogs 2026 Import Health Standard came into force on 1 July 2026, with a 9-month transition during which pets can still be imported under the 2021 standard. It recommends using a pet transport company, or following its 10-step process, and there is separate guidance for clearance from Australia and from other countries.","Dogs brought into New Zealand and later sent on to Australia must be tested for Leishmania infantum before entering Australia. The test must be done after the dog has lived in New Zealand for at least 21 days, and dogs cannot go to Australia until they have lived in New Zealand for more than 180 days. A positive dog is unlikely to be allowed to stay in New Zealand.","Questions go to animal.imports@mpi.govt.nz. MPI also has separate pages for assistance dogs, pets in transit, pets on vessels, and other pets such as rabbits or guinea pigs."]}
